## 5. Routing and BGP

BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) manages routing between your network and AWS.

### BGP Configuration

```bash
# Describe BGP peer details for VIF
aws directconnect describe-virtual-interfaces \
  --virtual-interface-id vif-12345678 \
  --query 'virtualInterfaces[0].bgpPeers'

# Response shows BGP session:
{
  "asn": 65000,
  "authKey": "authkey123",
  "addressFamily": "ipv4",
  "amazonAddress": "203.0.113.1",
  "customerAddress": "203.0.113.2",
  "bgpPeerState": "available",
  "bgpStatus": "up"
}

# BGP Session Parameters:
# - Your ASN: 65000 (private ASN: 64512-65534)
# - AWS ASN: 16509 (Amazon's public ASN)
# - Auth Key: MD5 password (optional)
# - Address Family: IPv4 or IPv6
```

### BGP Routing Example

```
Your Data Center:
├─ On-premises networks: 10.0.0.0/8
└─ BGP announces: 10.0.0.0/8 → AWS

AWS VPC:
├─ VPC CIDR: 172.31.0.0/16
├─ Subnets: 172.31.0.0/24, 172.31.1.0/24
└─ BGP announces: 172.31.0.0/16 → On-premises

Routing Table Update:
On-premises router:
  Destination: 172.31.0.0/16
  Next Hop: 203.0.113.1 (AWS side of BGP peer)
  Via: Direct Connect

AWS Route Table:
  Destination: 10.0.0.0/8
  Next Hop: vif-12345678 (Virtual Interface)
  Via: Direct Connect VIF
```

### BGP Failover

```bash
# Configure backup route via internet:
# Route 1: Direct Connect (preferred, lowest cost)
# Route 2: Internet VPN (backup if DX down)

# BGP configuration on customer router:
route-map PREFER-DX
  set local-preference 200
  
route-map PREFER-VPN
  set local-preference 100

# Result:
# Normal: Traffic via Direct Connect (lowest cost, best performance)
# DX Down: Traffic fails over to VPN (within seconds)
# DX Recovered: Traffic moves back (automatic)
```

