## 6. Resilience and Failover

### High Availability Design

```
Option 1: Single Connection (No Redundancy)
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│ Data Center                     │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Direct Connect: 10 Gbps         │
│ Status: Up = 10 Gbps            │
│ Status: Down = 0 Gbps (outage!) │
└─────────────────────────────────┘

Option 2: LAG (Same Location Redundancy)
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Data Center                          │
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│ DX Connection 1 (10 Gbps)            │
│ DX Connection 2 (10 Gbps) ──→ LAG   │
│ Status: One down = 10 Gbps           │
│ Status: Both down = 0 Gbps (rare)    │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘

Option 3: Multi-Location (Maximum Redundancy)
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Data Center                          │
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ├─ DX to Equinix SV1 (10 Gbps)      │
│ ├─ DX to Digital Realty SV (10 Gbps)│
│ └─ Internet VPN Backup (backup)     │
│                                     │
│ Status: Any 1 connection = 10 Gbps  │
│ Status: All down = VPN backup       │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Failover Configuration

```bash
# Scenario: Primary DX fails, failover to backup

# 1. Configure BGP with weight/priority
neighbor 203.0.113.1 route-map PREFER-DX in
neighbor 203.0.113.2 route-map PREFER-VPN in

route-map PREFER-DX
  set local-preference 200

route-map PREFER-VPN
  set local-preference 100

# 2. Monitor connections
aws directconnect describe-connection-loa \
  --connection-id dxcon-12345678 \
  --query 'loa'

# 3. Set up CloudWatch alarms
aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm \
  --alarm-name DX-Connection-Down \
  --metric-name ConnectionState \
  --namespace AWS/DirectConnect \
  --statistic Average \
  --period 60 \
  --threshold 1 \
  --comparison-operator LessThanThreshold \
  --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:us-west-1:123456789012:dx-alerts

# 4. Test failover (without affecting production):
# Disable primary connection → traffic moves to backup
# Monitor traffic patterns → confirm failover works
# Re-enable primary → traffic moves back
```

